# Line endings, and one of these is load-bearing.
#
# This machine has `core.autocrlf=true`, so without a rule here git checks
# everything out with CRLF. For source that is harmless. For the files below it
# is not.

* text=auto eol=lf

# **The goldens and the recorded oracle are compared byte for byte**, against
# strings this program builds with `\n`. Checked out with CRLF they differ from
# the render on every line, and the failure reads as "the report drifted" on a
# fresh clone that has changed nothing. `-text` disables conversion in both
# directions, so what is committed is what lands on disk.
crates/tga-report/tests/golden.html           -text
crates/tga-report/tests/synthetic/stats.json  -text
crates/tga-report/tests/synthetic/result.json -text
crates/tga-report/tests/synthetic/events.json -text
crates/tga-report/tests/fixture.stats.json    -text

# **The stylesheet and the script are copied into the report verbatim**, so they
# are the same case as the goldens one step earlier: checked out with CRLF, every
# report this program writes differs from the committed golden on 500 lines, and
# nothing in the source has changed. They are `include_str!`d, which is exactly
# why they need this and an ordinary `.css` would not.
crates/tga-report/assets/report.css -text
crates/tga-report/assets/report.js  -text

# **cmd's `goto` cannot find a label in an LF-only batch file.** It fails with
# "The system cannot find the batch label specified" partway through a run that
# has already done its work — which is how `save.bat` first shipped.
*.bat text eol=crlf

# Never touch the typefaces. A CRLF conversion inside a TTF is a font that no
# longer parses, and the failure surfaces as a window in the wrong typeface with
# no error anywhere.
*.ttf binary

# The icon, for the same reason: `* text=auto` would treat a run of BMP bytes
# that happens to look like text as text, and a CRLF inside a DIB is an icon
# that draws as garbage in Explorer and nowhere else.
*.ico binary
